//BUG="whether a for variable aliases the container depends on unrelated statements"
//OUT="9 8"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `for it items` lowers to `it = $iter.next()`, and
// `_VecIter.next` returns `self.vec[self.i - 1]` — a reference into the
// vector. Nothing pins `it` to a value or to a reference, so the AutoRef
// around `next()`'s result is left for the type resolver, which settles it
// from however the whole function happens to use `it`.
//
// The result is that the same write means two different things:
//
//   * In the first loop below, `it` is only ever used for field access, so it
//     stays `&P` and `it.v = 9` writes through into the vector.
//   * In the second loop, `read(it)` takes a `P` by value. That forces `it` to
//     be a `P`, so `it.v = 8` now writes to a per-iteration copy and is
//     silently discarded — even though the write is *above* the call that
//     changed its meaning.
//
// So adding a line that only reads the loop variable retroactively turns an
// earlier write into a no-op, with no diagnostic. Today this prints "9 9":
// the second loop's write is lost.
//
// Which of the two is right is the language's call — `Vec.operator[]` returns
// `&T` on purpose so that `v[0] = 7` works, which argues for the aliasing
// reading used here. If the intended semantics is instead that `for` hands out
// copies, the fix is the other way round and this case should expect "1 1"
// with the first loop's write dropped too. What cannot stand is the two
// disagreeing.

struct P
    v: i32

fn read p: P
    ret p.v

fn main
    items = Vec[P]()
    items.push(P v 1)

    for it items
        it.v = 9
    first = items[0].v

    for it items
        it.v = 8
        z = read(it)
    second = items[0].v

    out "{first} {second}"
